tsdb portable binaries for x86-64 Linux
=======================================

Built by `make dist` (on Ubuntu with GCC 15 or 16), for running on other
distributions, AlmaLinux 10 first of all.

Contents
  bin/tsdbd            the server
  bin/tsdb             the command-line tool (create, insert, import, query, stats)
  bench/               codec-bench, type-bench, tsdb-bench, insert-bench, tsdbc-bench, scale-bench
  lib/libtsdb.a        the embedded engine; link with -lzstd -lpthread -lm
  lib/libtsdbclient.a  the C client of tsdbd; link with -lzstd -lpthread -lm
  include/             tsdb.h, tsdb_protocol.h, tsdb_wire.h, tsdb_calendar.h, tsdb_client.h
  tests/               the test suite of this build; tests/run-tests.sh runs it (libcrashfs.so there is a
                       test-only shim that fails a write on purpose; nothing else loads it)
  systemd/tsdbd.service
  systemd/tsdbd.properties   the settings the unit reads (/etc/tsdb/tsdbd.properties)
  BUILDINFO            version, build id, the source repository, zstd and liburing versions, compiler, glibc
  LICENSE, NOTICE      the server, the engine (lib/libtsdb.a) and the tools: GNU AGPL, version 3 only
  LICENSES/, LICENSING.md
                       the Apache License 2.0 text, which covers the C client (lib/libtsdbclient.a) and the
                       headers in include/ (a header's licence does not change that of lib/libtsdb.a: a program
                       that links libtsdb is under the AGPL, one that only uses libtsdbclient.a is not);
                       LICENSING.md explains the split and the commercial licence
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES  zstd and liburing are linked into the binaries: their licenses

Source code (GNU AGPL, version 3, section 6)
  The Corresponding Source of this tarball is the tsdb repository at the build id in BUILDINFO:
    https://github.com/IntelliStream-DataHub/intellistream-tsdb
  (`git checkout` the tag vX.Y.Z of the release, or the commit after the "-gCOMMIT" of a build id; `make dist`
  there builds this tarball). The binaries also contain two libraries that `make dist` links in statically, in the
  versions BUILDINFO names; their sources are upstream's tags of those versions:
    zstd      https://github.com/facebook/zstd          (tag vVERSION)
    liburing  https://github.com/axboe/liburing         (tag liburing-VERSION)
  Whoever passes the tarball on must offer the same (or this text, unchanged).

Requirements
  CPU     x86-64-v2: SSE4.2 and POPCNT, AVX not needed (Intel Nehalem and
          later, Sandy Bridge included; AMD Bulldozer and later). Both the x86-64-v3 (default) and
          the x86-64-v2 builds of AlmaLinux 10 run it.
  glibc   2.34 or newer (AlmaLinux 10 has 2.39, EL9 has 2.34). zstd and liburing
          are linked in; nothing else is needed.
  kernel  io_uring for tsdbd (AlmaLinux 10: kernel 6.12). If tsdbd stops with
          "io_uring: Operation not permitted" or "Function not implemented",
          check `sysctl kernel.io_uring_disabled` (0 allows it) and, in a
          container, the seccomp profile.

Speed on new CPUs
  The baseline is x86-64-v2, but the code that decodes, encodes and folds
  values is in the binary three times: for x86-64-v2, x86-64-v3 (AVX2, BMI2,
  LZCNT) and x86-64-v4 (AVX-512). The best one this CPU has is picked at
  startup; tsdbd prints it ("simd avx2, codecs x86-64-v3"). Every level writes
  byte-identical parts and gives identical query results, so parts move freely
  between machines. TSDB_CPU=v2 (or v3) runs the lower level on a newer CPU,
  to see how an older machine will do.

Install
  tar xzf tsdb-VERSION-x86_64-linux.tar.gz && cd tsdb-VERSION-x86_64-linux
  tests/run-tests.sh /var/tmp            # optional, a few minutes
  install -m 755 bin/tsdbd bin/tsdb /usr/local/bin/
  restorecon -v /usr/local/bin/tsdbd /usr/local/bin/tsdb    # SELinux: bin_t
  useradd -r -s /sbin/nologin -d /var/lib/tsdb tsdb
  install -d -o tsdb -g tsdb -m 750 /var/lib/tsdb
  runuser -u tsdb -- /usr/local/bin/tsdb /var/lib/tsdb user add admin --superuser   # asks for a password
  install -d /etc/tsdb && install -m 644 systemd/tsdbd.properties /etc/tsdb/
  install -m 644 systemd/tsdbd.service /etc/systemd/system/
  systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl enable --now tsdbd

  The unit runs tsdbd on 127.0.0.1:7070 with its data in /var/lib/tsdb, as
  /etc/tsdb/tsdbd.properties says (a .properties file, one dotted key per setting;
  `tsdbd --config-keys` lists them, docs/config.md in the source explains them;
  `tsdbd --config FILE --check-config` checks a file; a change needs a restart).
  Clients log in with a user name and a password. tsdbd does not start until
  the database has a superuser (a user who may do everything): that is the
  `user add ... --superuser` line. More users and their rights:
  `tsdb /var/lib/tsdb user` lists the commands, which also work while tsdbd
  runs. Run as root, tsdb first becomes the owner of the database directory
  (the tsdb user) for good, for every command, and says so: the tsdb user
  could put links in the directory that root would follow. So USERS (mode
  0600) and every other file it writes belong to tsdb, and files named on
  its command line (--password-file, export -o) are read and written as tsdb
  too: use TSDB_PASSWORD, < and > for files only root may read. USERS holds no
  passwords, but keep it as secret as the data (SECURITY.md says why). Without
  TLS, data and queries travel in the clear: listen on a trusted interface only
  (SECURITY.md in the source). Under SELinux it
  runs unconfined (unconfined_service_t): no tsdb policy exists, so none
  confines it; the systemd sandboxing in the unit (ProtectSystem=strict, ...)
  is what limits it.
